NAME
       tune2fs - show and change settings of an ext2, ext3 or ext4 file system

SYNOPSIS
       tune2fs [-l] [-L LABEL] [-U UUID] [-c COUNT] [-i INTERVAL] [-m PERCENT]
               [-o [^]MOUNT-OPTION] [-O [^]FEATURE] DEVICE

DESCRIPTION
       tune2fs changes settings stored in the superblock of an ext file
       system. Most changes may be made while the file system is mounted.
       For XFS file systems use xfs_admin instead.

OPTIONS
       -l     List the superblock: label, UUID, features, block and inode
              counts, default mount options, mount count and check interval.

       -L LABEL
              Set the file system label (up to 16 characters), used by
              LABEL= in /etc/fstab.

       -U UUID
              Set a new UUID: a value, random or time. The file system should
              be unmounted.

       -c COUNT
              Check the file system after COUNT mounts (-1 turns it off).

       -i INTERVAL
              Check after this much time, such as 30d or 6m (0 turns it off).

       -m PERCENT
              Space reserved for root (5% by default).

       -o OPTION
              Set default mount options, such as acl or user_xattr (^ in front
              removes one).

       -O FEATURE
              Turn a file system feature on (or off with ^).

EXAMPLES
       Give the ext4 file system on /dev/vdb1 the label data:

           tune2fs -L data /dev/vdb1

       Look at its settings:

           tune2fs -l /dev/vdb1 | grep -i -e name -e uuid -e 'mount options'

SEE ALSO
       mkfs.ext4(8), e2label(8), xfs_admin(8), blkid(8), fstab(5)
